      ":question/id": "fa5b7031-20b6-5e99-a11b-d898565b8324",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, then $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right) &=\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot 5+k=k+25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=k+25.\n$$",
